WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.234
I'm honored that you're here.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:10.067
You changed music for all of us
with your magazine.

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.400
What year was it? 196--
-1967.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.734
And rock and roll was
a really small thing then.

00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:15.901
The Beatles, of course,
were out

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:17.067
and the Stones were out
and Dylan was out.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:18.434
But it wasn't anything
like today.

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:20.601
And we just kind of helped
bring it to the world.

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:21.934
Say, "Hey, this stuff is great."

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.901
-But no one took music
seriously.

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:26.234
-Yeah.
And we were the first people

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:27.901
to come along
and really write about it

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:31.400
as kind of a very artistic,
very meaningful,

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.567
very important thing that
communicated with young people,

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:36.934
and that was going to be
the, you know,

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:38.534
sound heard around the world.
-Yeah.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:41.400
-And we saw how much
social meaning it had

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.467
and how much it conveyed ideas
and feelings

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:48.601
and the way people
should treat each other.

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:50.434
It had a real message.

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:52.534
-The first cover
was John Lennon.

00:00:52.534 --> 00:00:54.734
If I -- If I -- now,
because I'm a fan of yours

00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:55.834
and I'm kind of a student.

00:00:55.834 --> 00:00:58.000
But the first one
was John Lennon.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.834
Was it "How I Won the War"?
-"How I Won the War."

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.167
That movie.
-He was dressed in a --

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.100
Yeah.
And he was doing a movie.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.834
So, that's the cover -- you got
John Lennon for the first cover.

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:08.000
This must have sold
a million copies.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.434
-It sold 7-- We shipped
40,000 copies, that issue.

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:13.234
-Wow.
-We got 35,000 back

00:01:13.234 --> 00:01:15.234
in unopened boxes.

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.300
But really smart --
-So you sold 5,000 copies?

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.567
-If you had been there,
you would've sold a million.

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:21.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:22.701
But that's cool to get
John Lennon

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:24.234
to be the first cover.

00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:27.834
-Well, it was, in truth,
it was the cheapest --

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.067
it was the only publicity
still I could find.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.334
We started with nothing.

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:33.300
So our choices were limited
to what record companies

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.434
would give us free
or movies would give us free,

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.100
and that was the best
of the free ones.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.767
-Yeah.
-Now then, we did -- went on,

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.200
and he did
many covers after that.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.534
-Yeah, of course he did, yes.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:45.067
Here's you, here, in 1967.

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.133
-My wife, Jane.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:50.234
You went on to start
with John Lennon.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:52.167
You went on --
obviously every musician.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:54.467
You went on to world leaders.

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.467
Here's you with Obama.
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.334
-You said that Obama
gave you a little --

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:01.467
He gave you a little gossip

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:03.801
when you were interviewing
President Obama.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.534
-Well, I had just been
with Bono the night before.

00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:07.968
I said I was going down there.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.267
He said, "Oh, I was just --
I stayed the night two weeks ago

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.767
at the White House, you know."
-Yeah, Bono.

00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:14.767
-Bono, and --
with Barack, first names.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.367
[ Laughter ]
And I drank him --

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:18.767
and he says, you know,
we went mano a mano

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:21.434
with the drinks until like
2:00 or 3:00 in the morning.

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:22.934
-No.
-I just stayed with him.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.200
And I'm seeing Obama
the next day.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.834
So, I said, "You know,
I saw Bono last night.

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:28.901
He said that the --
he drank you under the table."

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.133
He says, "You're kidding.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:32.033
He -- I put him away."

00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:33.234
-What?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:35.434
-"I had to carry him
to the Lincoln bedroom."

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:37.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.167
-That's not true.
-It's the truth.

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:41.200
-President Obama said
that he carried Bono

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.634
to the Lincoln bedroom?
-Yeah.

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:44.534
Now, that's ver--
I'm not supposed to say that.

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:45.701
That's totally discreet.

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.000
-Yeah, of course.
Yeah.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.167
No one's watching.
Don't worry about it.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.133
No one's watching.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.634
-It's my reputation.
-Yeah.

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:54.000
You know, last night, you did
a cool thing here in New York.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.601
You got interviewed
by one of your old pals and --

00:02:57.601 --> 00:02:59.701
Bruce Springsteen.
-That was great.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:01.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.367
-I was going to ask you,
though, what's it like --

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:05.033
what's it like
being interviewed --

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:06.267
even like right now.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:08.067
But Bruce Springsteen,
you've interviewed him,

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:09.567
and your magazine has --

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.634
-He's not close to as good
as you are, firstly.

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:13.200
-Thank you.
I appreciate that.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.100
Do you hear that, Bruce?
You can't host a talk show.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.467
-But he does --
and also sings better.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:18.601
I mean, no, you sing
better than him.

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:19.634
Let's face it.
[ Laughs ]

00:03:19.634 --> 00:03:21.167
I've seen you play guitar.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.300
-Yeah, what was Bruce --
-Bruce is terrific.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.501
I mean, he is, first off,

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:25.634
one of the nicest people
that I've ever met.

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.033
-He is.

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:28.400
-And one of the most thoughtful
people you've ever met.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.901
And we've known
each other years and years.

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:31.901
We're quite good friends
by this time.

00:03:31.901 --> 00:03:35.133
And so, it was like a
conversation between old friends

00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.267
in which the audience
of about 900 people

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:39.400
got to kind of
watch and participate in.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.267
And Bruce did a great job
of interviewing.

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:44.501
-Can you tell the story
that you were telling me

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.434
about the --
well, he was all in shape,

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.234
and he said
he didn't want to be fat Elvis.

00:03:48.234 --> 00:03:49.934
-Oh, God.
Oh, yeah.

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:54.801
Yeah, we were -- he had, like,
whipped himself at some point.

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:57.100
I go into, like, this buff,
killer shape.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.634
-Oh, he's Jacked.
-And I said,

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:00.133
"Well, how'd you get --"
He says,

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:02.067
"You know, I was on concert --
on stage one night,

00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:03.434
and I looked up
at the screen behind me.

00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:05.133
And I looked at that guy
up there, and said,

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:06.567
'That's the fat Elvis.'"

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:08.133
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:10.367
So, then he got
totally in buff shape.

00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:12.334
Well, then, about three
or four months later,

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:14.434
we are in
Rio de Janeiro together.

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:15.901
We was playing Rock in Rio.

00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:18.100
And we go with our friends
and stuff like that.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.901
And we're walking on the beach.

00:04:19.901 --> 00:04:21.801
And apparently,
I didn't see them,

00:04:21.801 --> 00:04:24.634
but he saw these paparazzi,
you know, following us.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:26.534
So, we get to lunch.

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:28.067
Immediately on the phone,
there's these pictures

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:30.367
that appear from some
photo agency, you know,

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:33.267
where he is looking great...
-Yeah.

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:35.701
-...and I'm looking like my gut
is hanging out.

00:04:35.701 --> 00:04:38.200
[ Laughter ]
I said, "Thanks, pal."

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.634
I mean, thanks for --
he said, "I saw them there."

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:40.968
I said, "Well, thanks
for alerting me."

00:04:40.968 --> 00:04:42.534
-Thanks for warning me, yeah.

00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:44.467
You're the one worried about --
yeah, you're Bruce Springsteen.

00:04:44.467 --> 00:04:46.868
You're Jacked.

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:48.400
-But he had a very funny thing
to say about it.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.000
He says,
"Because of the rapidity

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.634
of which those pictures
appeared," he said,

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:55.467
"you can't go anywhere anymore
and then you're everywhere."

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:56.934
-Yeah, and you're everywhere.

00:04:56.934 --> 00:05:01.267
Yeah -- there's all sorts of fun
stories and photos in here.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.534
I like this picture of you --

00:05:03.534 --> 00:05:07.167
your family with Paul McCartney
and his family.

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:10.267
And I like this because
you have a funny story.

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:13.100
Theo, your son,
his nickname is Beetle.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.334
-Beetle, right.
-Yeah, Beetle -- B-E-E-T-L-E.

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:17.534
Right, sure?
And, yeah.

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:18.868
And so, in this picture,

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:21.968
he actually went and found
an actual beetle.

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:23.501
-And there's three beetles.

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:26.901
-Yeah, so Paul -- and actually,
he's holding an actual beetle.

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:28.701
-There's one other thing
that's important --

00:05:28.701 --> 00:05:31.000
interesting about
that besides that story,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.634
is that Linda used to be a
photographer for "Rolling Stone"

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:37.667
in the very early days,
1967 and '68.

00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:39.667
And I think it was
'68 or something,

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:41.767
she asked me if she could go
to England, shoot the Beatles.

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.601
I said,
"Why do you want to go?"

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:44.868
"I want to meet Paul."

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:47.234
So, we sent her.
-Wow.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:48.701
And the rest is history.
-Yeah.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:50.234
-Yeah, just saying,
no big deal.

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:51.701
-No big deal.
-Yeah, no big deal.

00:05:51.701 --> 00:05:54.701
Also --
-I now get so many royalties.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:56.501
Every time they play
"Yesterday"...

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:57.601
-You're the fifth Beatle.

00:05:57.601 --> 00:05:59.133
Yeah, yeah, of course.

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:00.267
And this is no big deal.

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:02.000
Just you sailing
with Mick Jagger.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.834
No big deal.
Everything's cool.

00:06:03.834 --> 00:06:06.000
Everything's fine.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.267
-You don't want my life.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:08.901
That's true.
-Yes, I do.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:10.968
Yes, we all do.
-This is a struggle.

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:13.868
-But I will say, I do like --
I do like hanging out with you.

00:06:13.868 --> 00:06:15.200
-Do you remember we hung out.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.567
-Yeah.
-We hung out with Mick.

00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:19.200
-Well, I had a party for him
at our house one night.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.934
And you were among the guests.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:22.534
-You had one of the best
parties I've ever been to.

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:24.434
-That was fun.
-Ever.

00:06:24.434 --> 00:06:25.701
Epic.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:28.601
-It's a terrible life I live.
I'm sorry.

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:31.100
-Jann Wenner throws a party.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:32.868
And everyone you can think of
is there.

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:34.868
I remember --
-So, you and Mick

00:06:34.868 --> 00:06:36.067
leave the party.

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:38.067
And suddenly, for some reason
on the stoop,

00:06:38.067 --> 00:06:39.934
you guys
start doing the dance-off,

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.601
you know,
of who can dance better.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.801
You, this one,
and Mick makes his move.

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:46.667
-Yeah, Mick Jagger challenged
me to a dance contest.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:47.868
"Dancing in the Street"!

00:06:47.868 --> 00:06:51.300
♪ Callin' out
around the world ♪

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.400
♪ Are you ready,
you ready, you ready ♪

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
It got loud.

00:06:57.934 --> 00:07:00.701
It got loud in the streets!

00:07:00.701 --> 00:07:01.801
In New York.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:02.767
-So, for every move
he's making,

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:04.167
Mick is trying to top him.

00:07:04.167 --> 00:07:05.834
Then it goes back.
He's doing it down.

00:07:05.834 --> 00:07:07.167
This goes on for about five --

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:09.467
Meanwhile, the entire
neighborhood gathered around,

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:10.701
looking at the --
-Yeah, it was like

00:07:10.701 --> 00:07:12.334
"West Side Story."
It was unbelievable.

00:07:12.334 --> 00:07:13.667
Yeah, we had a dance-off.
-It was brilliant.

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:15.801
I became mayor of the street
after that.

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:17.000
They loved me.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.734
-There's so many great stories
in here.

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:21.167
And, man,
what a life well-lived.

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:23.033
And thank you for everything
you've done for music

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:25.067
and just for being
a good friend to us.

00:07:25.067 --> 00:07:26.300
We love you.
Jann Wenner, right there.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:27.434 --> 00:07:30.100
"Like a Rolling Stone"
is out now.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.234
Go pick it up.
We'll be right back

00:07:31.234 --> 00:07:33.234
with a performance
from Little Big Town.

00:07:33.234 --> 00:07:34.968
Stick around, everybody.

